++  test-mc-gh1389-counting
  %+  expect-eq
    !>  ^-  wain
    :~
        'Error: Temporal property CountAtMostFour was violated.'
        'Error: The following behavior constitutes a counter-example:'
        'State 1: <Initial predicate>'
        'x = FALSE'
        'State 2: <Next line 24, col 9 to line 24, col 22 of module spec>'
        'x = TRUE'
        'State 3: <Next line 24, col 9 to line 24, col 22 of module spec>'
        'x = FALSE'
        'State 4: <Next line 24, col 9 to line 24, col 22 of module spec>'
        'x = TRUE'
        'State 5: <Next line 24, col 9 to line 24, col 22 of module spec>'
        'x = FALSE'
        'State 6: <Next line 24, col 9 to line 24, col 22 of module spec>'
        'x = TRUE'
        'State 7: <Next line 24, col 9 to line 24, col 22 of module spec>'
        'x = FALSE'
        'State 8: <Next line 24, col 9 to line 24, col 22 of module spec>'
        'x = TRUE'
        'State 9: <Next line 24, col 9 to line 24, col 22 of module spec>'
        'x = FALSE'
        'State 10: <Next line 24, col 9 to line 24, col 22 of module spec>'
        'x = TRUE'
        '5 states generated, 2 distinct states found, 0 states left on queue.'
        'The depth of the complete state graph search is 2.'
    ==
  !>
  %-  trim
  %+  check:check
    '--------------------------- MODULE spec ---------------------------\0a\\* Regression test for https://github.com/tlaplus/tlaplus/issues/1389\0a\\* exercising the "counting safety property" use case -- the primary\0a\\* motivation for permitting RECURSIVE operators at temporal level.\0a\\*\0a\\* Bound the number of times an event may occur.  The canonical example\0a\\* here is "x is TRUE in at most n disjoint intervals", equivalently \0a\\* "x becomes TRUE at most n times":\0a\\*\0a\\*   AtMost(0) == []~x                              \\* never TRUE\0a\\*   AtMost(1) == [](x => [](~x => []~x))           \\* TRUE in at most 1 interval\0a\\*   AtMost(2) == [](x => [](~x => [](x => [](~x => []~x))))\0a\\*   ...\0a\0aEXTENDS Naturals, FiniteSets, TLCExt\0a\0aVARIABLE x\0a\0aRECURSIVE AtMost(_)\0aAtMost(n) == IF n = 0 THEN []~x\0a                      ELSE [](x => [](~x => AtMost(n - 1)))\0a\0aInit == x = FALSE\0aNext == x\' \\in BOOLEAN\0aSpec == Init /\\ [][Next]_x\0a\0aCountAtMostFour == AtMost(4)\0a\0a------------------------------------------------------------------\0a\0aPostCondition ==\0a    LET S == CounterExample.state\0a        A == CounterExample.action\0a    IN\0a    /\\ Cardinality(S) = 10\0a    /\\ Cardinality(A) = 9\0a    /\\ \\A i \\in 1..10 :\0a         \\E s \\in S :\0a            /\\ s[1] = i\0a            /\\ s[2].x = (i % 2 = 0)\0a    /\\ \\A a \\in A : a[2].name = "Next"\0a==================================================================\0a'
  'SPECIFICATION Spec\0aCHECK_DEADLOCK FALSE\0aPROPERTIES\0a    CountAtMostFour\0a'
::
::  APPROVED DIFFERENCE (R2.7.6.1) -- this arm asserts the PORT's output,
::  not the pin's. NOT a cap case, measured: the recursion is guarded by a STATE variable,
::  so the expansion fails on its first attempt and never recurses --
::  re-running with `+expand-cap` lowered to 3 leaves this output
::  byte-identical. The port refuses the translation with the pin's own EC
::  2213 text; tlc2 attempts it and dies on the stack
::  the pin (out.txt): Error: This was a Java StackOverflowError. It was probably the result /
::  of an incorrect recursive function definition that caused TLC to enter /
::  an infinite loop when trying to compute the function or its application
::  / to an element in its putative domain.
++  test-mc-gh1389-guard
  %+  expect-eq
    !>  ^-  wain
    :~
        'Error: TLC cannot handle the temporal formula line 12, col 9 to line 12, col 13 of module spec'
    ==
  !>
  %-  trim
  %+  check:check
    '---- MODULE spec ----\0a\\* upstream test-model/Github1389StateGuard.tla: the recursive operator\'s\0a\\* parameter is bound to a state variable, so the IF guard never resolves\0a\\* during tableau construction and the expansion does not terminate.\0aEXTENDS Naturals\0aVARIABLE x\0aRECURSIVE op(_)\0aop(n) == IF n = 0 THEN <>(x = 0) ELSE op(n - 1)\0aInit == x = 0\0aNext == x\' \\in 0..3\0aSpec == Init /\\ [][Next]_x\0aProp == op(x)\0a====\0a'
  'SPECIFICATION Spec\0aCHECK_DEADLOCK FALSE\0aPROPERTY Prop\0a'
::
::  APPROVED DIFFERENCE (R2.7.6.1) -- this arm asserts the PORT's output,
::  not the pin's. the expansion genuinely runs away (guard FALSE, ELSE re-invokes with the
::  same argument), so `+expand-cap` stops it at 1.000 nested expansions and
::  `+user-op` PROPAGATES that rejection rather than swallowing it -- a Hoon
::  runaway is an uncatchable hang, not a throwable, the same trade
::  `live-graph-cap` makes
::  the pin (out.txt): Error: This was a Java StackOverflowError. It was probably the result /
::  of an incorrect recursive function definition that caused TLC to enter /
::  an infinite loop when trying to compute the function or its application
::  / to an element in its putative domain.
++  test-mc-gh1389-loops
  %+  expect-eq
    !>  ^-  wain
    :~
        'Error: expansion bound reached (1000 nested user-operator expansions) translating the temporal formula line 7, col 37 to line 7, col 42 of module spec; the recursion reaches no base case. tlc2 has no bound here: it overflows the JVM stack and reports EC 1005'
    ==
  !>
  %-  trim
  %+  check:check
    '---- MODULE spec ----\0a\\* upstream test-model/Github1389Loops.tla: the expansion does NOT reach a\0a\\* base case (the guard is FALSE and the ELSE re-invokes with the same\0a\\* argument). tlc2 overflows the JVM stack and reports EC 1005.\0aVARIABLE x\0aRECURSIVE bad(_)\0abad(u) == IF u THEN [][TRUE]_x ELSE bad(u)\0aSpec == x = TRUE /\\ [][x\' = ~x]_x\0aProp == bad(FALSE)\0a====\0a'
  'SPECIFICATION Spec\0aCHECK_DEADLOCK FALSE\0aPROPERTY Prop\0a'
::
++  test-mc-gh1389-ok
  %+  expect-eq
    !>  ^-  wain
    :~
        'Model checking completed. No error has been found.'
        '3 states generated, 2 distinct states found, 0 states left on queue.'
        'The depth of the complete state graph search is 2.'
    ==
  !>
  %-  trim
  %+  check:check
    '---- MODULE spec ----\0a\\* upstream test-model/Github1389.tla (issue #1389): RECURSIVE operators\0a\\* whose body reaches temporal level, expanded by Liveness.astToLive with\0a\\* only the constant-guard IF branch translated. Three shapes: a boolean\0a\\* guard, a numeric guard walked down to 0, and the recursion in THEN.\0aEXTENDS Naturals\0aVARIABLE x\0aRECURSIVE ap_0(_)\0aap_0(u) == IF u THEN <>(x = TRUE) ELSE ap_0(~u)\0aRECURSIVE ap_2(_)\0aap_2(n) == IF n = 0 THEN <>(x = TRUE) ELSE ap_2(n - 1)\0aRECURSIVE ap_3(_)\0aap_3(n) == IF n > 0 THEN ap_3(n - 1) ELSE <>(x = TRUE)\0aSpec == x = TRUE /\\ [][x\' = ~x]_x\0aPropIf      == ap_0(TRUE)\0aPropDepth   == ap_2(3)\0aPropElseRec == ap_3(3)\0a====\0a'
  'SPECIFICATION Spec\0aCHECK_DEADLOCK FALSE\0aPROPERTIES\0a    PropIf\0a    PropDepth\0a    PropElseRec\0a'
